1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,290
Imagine you're in a lift.
You press the button.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:05,200
The doors slide shut.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:06,650
The lift starts moving.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,190
Everything is perfectly normal.

5
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:11,370
Until, one day, it isn't.

6
00:00:11,981 --> 00:00:13,601
The lift stops between floors.

7
00:00:13,681 --> 00:00:15,591
So you press the emergency button.

8
00:00:16,281 --> 00:00:17,851
You expect someone to answer.

9
00:00:18,081 --> 00:00:19,611
But nobody does.

10
00:00:20,423 --> 00:00:22,253
Not because the button is broken.

11
00:00:22,723 --> 00:00:25,313
Not because the lift has no electricity.

12
00:00:26,303 --> 00:00:29,323
But because the mobile network
that the emergency phone uses...

13
00:00:29,403 --> 00:00:31,493
has been switched off.

14
00:00:32,256 --> 00:00:36,286
Welcome to the surprisingly
complicated problem of turning off 2G.

15
00:00:37,045 --> 00:00:41,155
Because 2G isn't just something your
old Nokia used twenty years ago.

16
00:00:42,005 --> 00:00:45,515
It is still quietly sitting inside
an astonishing number of things.

17
00:00:46,381 --> 00:00:51,891
Things we generally expect to work for
decades. Things like lifts. Alarms.

18
00:00:51,981 --> 00:00:54,971
Industrial equipment. Meters. Cars.

19
00:00:55,848 --> 00:00:56,868
And, rather awkwardly...

20
00:00:56,948 --> 00:00:58,738
emergency systems.

21
00:01:01,377 --> 00:01:03,487
Let's start with the obvious question.

22
00:01:04,437 --> 00:01:06,487
Why are we still talking about 2G?

23
00:01:07,377 --> 00:01:12,587
GSM first appeared commercially
in the early 1990s. It is ancient.

24
00:01:13,497 --> 00:01:16,207
By technology standards,
it is practically a fossil.

25
00:01:17,119 --> 00:01:18,539
Today we have 4G.

26
00:01:18,619 --> 00:01:20,089
5G.

27
00:01:20,799 --> 00:01:22,569
Gigabit mobile connections.

28
00:01:22,959 --> 00:01:26,789
We can stream 4K video from a
phone while sitting on a train.

29
00:01:27,610 --> 00:01:32,270
So surely it makes sense to get rid
of this ancient technology? Well...

30
00:01:32,350 --> 00:01:35,110
For your smartphone, probably. For a lift?

31
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:36,540
Not so much.

32
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:42,790
A lift doesn't need 5G.
It doesn't need Netflix.

33
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:45,190
It doesn't need a gigabit connection.

34
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:47,690
It doesn't even need the internet.

35
00:01:48,630 --> 00:01:51,610
It just needs to be
able to say this. "Help.

36
00:01:51,690 --> 00:01:55,140
I'm stuck between floors."
That's basically it.

37
00:01:55,855 --> 00:01:58,595
An emergency communicator
in a lift might simply

38
00:01:58,675 --> 00:02:01,425
establish a voice call
with a service centre.

39
00:02:02,455 --> 00:02:05,485
And GSM is perfectly
capable of doing that.

40
00:02:06,257 --> 00:02:08,467
The same applies to many alarm systems.

41
00:02:09,397 --> 00:02:13,687
Some industrial equipment. Some
tracking devices. Some meters.

42
00:02:14,635 --> 00:02:16,325
They don't need enormous bandwidth.

43
00:02:17,175 --> 00:02:20,145
They need a tiny, reliable
communication channel.

44
00:02:20,975 --> 00:02:24,605
And that's exactly the sort of
thing GSM was rather good at.

45
00:02:26,875 --> 00:02:28,525
And then there was GPRS.

46
00:02:29,275 --> 00:02:32,445
If you were around in the early
2000s, you may remember it.

47
00:02:33,435 --> 00:02:35,975
GPRS was basically mobile data over GSM.

48
00:02:36,055 --> 00:02:37,195
And it was...

49
00:02:37,275 --> 00:02:40,105
slow. Very slow.

50
00:02:40,937 --> 00:02:43,607
We are talking about tens
of kilobits per second.

51
00:02:44,577 --> 00:02:48,527
Today, a teenager would probably
describe that as a network outage.

52
00:02:49,441 --> 00:02:51,291
But some of us actually used it.

53
00:02:52,061 --> 00:02:54,121
There were mobile data
packages that allowed

54
00:02:54,201 --> 00:02:57,451
you to use GPRS as your
internet connection.

55
00:02:58,285 --> 00:03:00,555
Yes. As your actual internet connection.

56
00:03:01,305 --> 00:03:02,945
Some of us knew no better. And somehow...

57
00:03:03,025 --> 00:03:04,535
we survived.

58
00:03:05,496 --> 00:03:07,816
The interesting thing is that GPRS is

59
00:03:07,896 --> 00:03:10,206
still perfectly adequate
for some machines.

60
00:03:11,336 --> 00:03:14,166
A device might only need to
send a few hundred bytes.

61
00:03:15,087 --> 00:03:18,447
Something like this. Temperature, 21.4.

62
00:03:18,787 --> 00:03:21,047
Pressure, 1012.

63
00:03:21,127 --> 00:03:23,667
Status, OK. That's it.

64
00:03:23,747 --> 00:03:25,407
You don't need 5G for that.

65
00:03:25,487 --> 00:03:28,897
You barely need what we'd
normally call the internet.

66
00:03:31,327 --> 00:03:33,337
And this is where things get interesting.

67
00:03:34,407 --> 00:03:36,637
2G isn't just used by old phones.

68
00:03:36,947 --> 00:03:38,817
It is used by machines.

69
00:03:39,811 --> 00:03:43,701
And machines have a very
annoying habit. If they work...

70
00:03:43,811 --> 00:03:45,781
nobody replaces them.

71
00:03:46,446 --> 00:03:49,736
A lift can stay in service
for twenty or thirty years.

72
00:03:50,606 --> 00:03:53,156
An industrial machine
can last even longer.

73
00:03:53,925 --> 00:03:57,375
An alarm system might be installed
and then forgotten about.

74
00:03:58,245 --> 00:04:02,535
A meter can sit somewhere for fifteen
years, quietly reporting its readings.

75
00:04:03,538 --> 00:04:07,338
And nobody is going to replace a
perfectly working device just because

76
00:04:07,418 --> 00:04:11,148
the mobile industry has moved on to
another generation of technology.

77
00:04:12,578 --> 00:04:14,768
Why would they? It works.

78
00:04:17,598 --> 00:04:19,308
Now let's talk about cars.

79
00:04:20,218 --> 00:04:23,248
Modern cars contain an
extraordinary amount of electronics.

80
00:04:24,395 --> 00:04:29,765
And some older cars have emergency
communication systems based on 2G or 3G.

81
00:04:30,755 --> 00:04:32,705
This includes older versions of eCall.

82
00:04:33,815 --> 00:04:38,125
The European emergency call system
can automatically contact 112

83
00:04:38,255 --> 00:04:42,405
after a serious accident and transmit
information about the vehicle.

84
00:04:43,595 --> 00:04:44,795
That's rather useful.

85
00:04:45,689 --> 00:04:50,029
But if the car's communication module
only understands an old mobile network...

86
00:04:50,109 --> 00:04:54,559
then switching off that network
creates a rather strange situation.

87
00:04:55,586 --> 00:05:00,226
The car still works. The engine works.
The brakes work. The airbags work.

88
00:05:00,306 --> 00:05:01,596
The radio works.

89
00:05:02,385 --> 00:05:04,385
You can drive it perfectly normally.

90
00:05:04,465 --> 00:05:09,395
But its emergency communication system
may no longer be able to communicate.

91
00:05:10,461 --> 00:05:12,351
The car hasn't become obsolete.

92
00:05:13,081 --> 00:05:14,691
The network it depends on has.

93
00:05:17,621 --> 00:05:20,911
Now, the obvious answer is this. "Fine.

94
00:05:21,521 --> 00:05:23,981
Just replace the old module." And

95
00:05:24,061 --> 00:05:26,491
sometimes that is
exactly what will happen.

96
00:05:27,217 --> 00:05:31,267
But it isn't always that simple.
Imagine thousands of lifts.

97
00:05:31,477 --> 00:05:33,627
Thousands of alarm systems.

98
00:05:34,527 --> 00:05:37,757
Industrial equipment
installed fifteen years ago.

99
00:05:38,107 --> 00:05:40,677
Vehicles that are perfectly roadworthy.

100
00:05:41,307 --> 00:05:44,017
Meters installed across an entire country.

101
00:05:44,739 --> 00:05:49,989
Who replaces all those devices? The
manufacturer? The building owner?

102
00:05:50,439 --> 00:05:53,029
The lift maintenance
company? The car owner?

103
00:05:53,695 --> 00:05:57,385
And what happens if the manufacturer
doesn't even make the device anymore?

104
00:05:58,375 --> 00:06:01,295
Suddenly, a very cheap
GSM modem has become

105
00:06:01,375 --> 00:06:04,205
a rather expensive engineering project.

106
00:06:06,645 --> 00:06:10,395
Now, there is a perfectly good
reason for wanting to switch off 2G.

107
00:06:11,245 --> 00:06:13,015
Radio spectrum is limited.

108
00:06:13,985 --> 00:06:16,135
Operators have only so much of it.

109
00:06:16,857 --> 00:06:19,967
Modern networks can use that
spectrum much more efficiently.

110
00:06:21,077 --> 00:06:26,167
LTE and 5G can provide vastly more
capacity and much better performance.

111
00:06:27,216 --> 00:06:30,276
So from the operator's point
of view, keeping an entire

112
00:06:30,356 --> 00:06:33,426
old network running forever
doesn't make much sense.

113
00:06:34,288 --> 00:06:36,868
Why maintain old equipment
and dedicate spectrum

114
00:06:36,948 --> 00:06:40,018
to a technology that most
people no longer use?

115
00:06:40,968 --> 00:06:43,098
That's a perfectly reasonable question.

116
00:06:43,922 --> 00:06:45,532
But there is another question.

117
00:06:46,182 --> 00:06:48,352
How much 2G do we actually need?

118
00:06:51,032 --> 00:06:52,842
Maybe the answer isn't this.

119
00:06:53,512 --> 00:06:57,502
"Keep the entire 2G network
forever." Maybe it's this.

120
00:06:57,912 --> 00:06:59,602
"Keep a tiny part of it."

121
00:07:00,286 --> 00:07:03,636
We don't need 2G to carry
millions of smartphone users.

122
00:07:04,546 --> 00:07:06,396
We don't need it for streaming video.

123
00:07:06,926 --> 00:07:09,416
We don't need it for
normal mobile internet.

124
00:07:10,285 --> 00:07:12,375
We don't even need huge
amounts of bandwidth.

125
00:07:13,425 --> 00:07:16,515
We might only need a small
number of GSM channels.

126
00:07:17,445 --> 00:07:22,375
Enough for voice, SMS, basic
GPRS data and emergency systems.

127
00:07:23,206 --> 00:07:26,616
Most of the spectrum could
still go to 4G and 5G.

128
00:07:27,086 --> 00:07:30,176
The old network would
become a tiny legacy layer.

129
00:07:32,546 --> 00:07:35,756
And it wouldn't even have to
belong to one particular operator.

130
00:07:36,726 --> 00:07:41,016
It doesn't have to be Vodafone.
Or O2. Or Deutsche Telekom.

131
00:07:42,376 --> 00:07:46,536
Imagine there was a shared European
legacy service. Let's call it...

132
00:07:46,616 --> 00:07:48,006
EU Legacy.

133
00:07:48,895 --> 00:07:51,165
A lift doesn't care who owns the tower.

134
00:07:51,995 --> 00:07:55,445
It just needs its old modem to
find the network and make the call.

135
00:07:56,225 --> 00:07:58,495
So perhaps the real problem isn't this.

136
00:07:59,245 --> 00:08:02,675
"We don't have enough
capacity." Perhaps it's this.

137
00:08:03,145 --> 00:08:05,595
"We don't want to reserve
any spectrum for it."

138
00:08:08,265 --> 00:08:10,015
And here's where it gets strange.

139
00:08:10,885 --> 00:08:12,775
This isn't entirely hypothetical.

140
00:08:13,867 --> 00:08:16,137
Regulators are aware of the problem.

141
00:08:17,127 --> 00:08:22,217
In May 2026, the European Commission
published a study on exactly this.

142
00:08:22,607 --> 00:08:25,917
What happens to the machines
when the old networks go.

143
00:08:26,682 --> 00:08:28,312
And the numbers are not small.

144
00:08:29,242 --> 00:08:31,632
If the networks are switched
off without coordination,

145
00:08:32,122 --> 00:08:35,192
64 million vehicles across
the EU could lose eCall.

146
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:39,030
64 million. So the problem isn't this.

147
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:41,930
"Somebody wants to keep their old Nokia."

148
00:08:43,079 --> 00:08:44,669
We're talking about infrastructure.

149
00:08:44,799 --> 00:08:48,169
Safety systems. Vehicles.

150
00:08:48,319 --> 00:08:49,719
Industrial equipment.

151
00:08:49,799 --> 00:08:52,719
And millions of devices
whose owners may not

152
00:08:52,799 --> 00:08:55,909
even realise which mobile
technology they use.

153
00:08:56,633 --> 00:08:58,423
And what does that study recommend?

154
00:08:59,373 --> 00:09:03,183
Keep at least one circuit-switched
network alive until at least 2030.

155
00:09:04,262 --> 00:09:06,932
In other words... keep
a legacy network alive.

156
00:09:10,072 --> 00:09:11,702
And here's the funny thing.

157
00:09:12,532 --> 00:09:14,582
We've solved this sort of problem before.

158
00:09:15,432 --> 00:09:18,782
Think about the transition from
black-and-white television to colour.

159
00:09:19,516 --> 00:09:21,896
When colour television
arrived, we didn't say this.

160
00:09:21,976 --> 00:09:26,726
"Black-and-white televisions
are obsolete. Throw them away."

161
00:09:27,573 --> 00:09:30,533
The colour television standards
were designed so that older

162
00:09:30,613 --> 00:09:34,623
black-and-white receivers could
still display the original image.

163
00:09:35,473 --> 00:09:38,783
New televisions could extract the
additional colour information.

164
00:09:39,874 --> 00:09:42,644
One broadcast. Old equipment still works.

165
00:09:43,374 --> 00:09:45,244
New equipment gets the new features.

166
00:09:46,114 --> 00:09:48,984
That is what good backward
compatibility looks like.

167
00:09:51,294 --> 00:09:54,984
Now, to be fair, mobile networks
are much more complicated.

168
00:09:55,974 --> 00:10:00,004
GSM and LTE are not simply two
versions of the same technology.

169
00:10:00,971 --> 00:10:03,691
You can't just add a
little bit of LTE to a GSM

170
00:10:03,771 --> 00:10:08,081
signal and expect a 1998
Nokia to understand it.

171
00:10:08,531 --> 00:10:09,881
It won't.

172
00:10:10,498 --> 00:10:13,688
But that doesn't mean we have to
switch the old system off overnight.

173
00:10:14,458 --> 00:10:15,808
We could run both.

174
00:10:15,938 --> 00:10:18,668
Until the old equipment is finally gone.

175
00:10:21,138 --> 00:10:23,848
And perhaps that's the
most important point.

176
00:10:24,558 --> 00:10:27,788
Legacy technology isn't
necessarily bad technology.

177
00:10:28,998 --> 00:10:32,288
Sometimes it's simply old technology
that is still doing its job.

178
00:10:33,014 --> 00:10:37,384
A GSM modem doesn't become dangerous
because it is twenty years old.

179
00:10:37,974 --> 00:10:43,424
A lift doesn't stop being useful because
its emergency telephone uses GSM.

180
00:10:44,184 --> 00:10:46,284
An industrial machine
doesn't suddenly become

181
00:10:46,364 --> 00:10:50,834
obsolete because the telecom
industry has invented 5G.

182
00:10:51,597 --> 00:10:56,907
We have a tendency to think of technology
as a sequence. Old. Replace. New.

183
00:10:57,690 --> 00:11:00,340
But real infrastructure
doesn't work like that.

184
00:11:01,150 --> 00:11:03,700
Real infrastructure overlaps. For decades.

185
00:11:06,430 --> 00:11:09,300
And this is why the current
timetable is interesting.

186
00:11:10,350 --> 00:11:15,060
Some European operators are planning to
retire 2G around the end of this decade.

187
00:11:15,893 --> 00:11:17,603
That sounds like a long time.

188
00:11:17,733 --> 00:11:20,423
Until you remember what
we're talking about.

189
00:11:21,313 --> 00:11:25,883
A smartphone from 2021 is
already old by 2026 standards.

190
00:11:26,013 --> 00:11:29,983
But a lift from 2011?
That's barely middle-aged.

191
00:11:31,464 --> 00:11:34,334
An industrial machine from 2005?

192
00:11:35,164 --> 00:11:38,054
It might still have another
twenty years of useful life.

193
00:11:38,879 --> 00:11:40,079
That's the mismatch.

194
00:11:40,859 --> 00:11:44,969
Mobile networks change much faster
than the infrastructure that uses them.

195
00:11:47,639 --> 00:11:51,409
And perhaps the best solution isn't
some revolutionary new technology.

196
00:11:52,399 --> 00:11:54,449
Maybe it's something incredibly boring.

197
00:11:55,289 --> 00:11:59,159
Just one question. "Does it still
work?" Yes. Good. Leave it alone.

198
00:12:01,993 --> 00:12:05,603
Technology doesn't always have to mean
destroying the previous generation.

199
00:12:06,833 --> 00:12:09,873
Sometimes the smartest thing you
can do is build the new system...

200
00:12:09,953 --> 00:12:13,283
and leave a tiny door
open for the old one.

201
00:12:13,953 --> 00:12:15,743
Because somewhere out there is a lift.

202
00:12:16,333 --> 00:12:17,863
There is probably an alarm.

203
00:12:18,433 --> 00:12:20,503
There is probably an industrial machine.

204
00:12:21,333 --> 00:12:23,183
There is definitely an old car.

205
00:12:23,953 --> 00:12:28,503
And inside all of them there may be a
little GSM modem made fifteen years ago.

206
00:12:29,413 --> 00:12:30,903
Nobody thinks about it.

207
00:12:31,473 --> 00:12:33,143
Nobody talks about it.

208
00:12:33,824 --> 00:12:35,174
It doesn't stream video.

209
00:12:35,944 --> 00:12:37,164
It doesn't browse the web.

210
00:12:37,244 --> 00:12:39,674
It doesn't download apps. It has one job.

211
00:12:40,484 --> 00:12:45,084
To send a tiny message when something
goes wrong. Something like this. "Help.

212
00:12:45,164 --> 00:12:46,364
I'm here."

213
00:12:46,833 --> 00:12:48,033
And honestly...

214
00:12:48,193 --> 00:12:52,383
I think that's a pretty good reason
to keep a few GSM channels alive.

215
00:12:53,231 --> 00:12:56,051
Because the most impressive
technology isn't necessarily

216
00:12:56,131 --> 00:12:59,621
the one that replaces
everything that came before.

217
00:13:00,409 --> 00:13:02,989
Sometimes... it's the one
that lets the old stuff keep

218
00:13:03,069 --> 00:13:06,139
working until we're
actually ready to let it go.

219
00:13:06,863 --> 00:13:08,273
2G may be ancient.

220
00:13:08,683 --> 00:13:11,173
But perhaps the right question isn't this.

221
00:13:11,263 --> 00:13:13,653
"Why are we still using it?"

222
00:13:14,344 --> 00:13:18,654
Perhaps it's this. "What happens if
we turn it off before we're ready?"
